<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Lapethos</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 312 BCE (approx.) · capital Lapethos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">North Cypriot kingdom where Phoenician kings ruled a Greek population in the Classical age, until Ptolemy I deposed the last king in 312 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Lapethos lay on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lambousa">Lambousa</a>, facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Greek tradition said it was founded by Praxandros from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>. A city-kingdom by the 7th century BCE, it had close ties with the Phoenician coast.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In the 5th and 4th centuries BCE Lapethos was ruled by a dynasty with Phoenician names, such as Sidqmelek, who issued coins with Phoenician legends; Phoenician inscriptions from the town honour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melqart">Melqart</a> and other gods. The economy rested on farming, fishing and trade, and the city had a good harbour.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Its last king, Praxippos, supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a>, who removed him in 312 BCE and annexed the kingdom. Lapethos continued as a Ptolemaic and Roman town, later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lambousa">Lambousa</a>, abandoned after Arab raids in the 7th century CE.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1000 BCE – 312 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:39.80%;width:13.69%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 BCE – 312 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lapethos</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sidqmelek, Praxippos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Phoenician and Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Phoenician; Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cyprus">Cyprus</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lapethos, Lapithos</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-656&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lapethos" aria-label="Kingdom of Lapethos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5515 918 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Lapethos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M5772 1297h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5835 1174h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5835 1174h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Cyprus. <a href="/atlas?year=-656&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lapethos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

